{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1332734-dark-eras-imperial-rome-the-marble-court","id":1332734,"title":"[Dark Eras Imperial Rome] The Marble Court","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-08-26T14:37:37-07:00","id":1332734,"title":"[Dark Eras Imperial Rome] The Marble Court","content":"\u200bHello folks! I'm currently working on a Play by Post Changeling game here on the forums. The game itself is set in Imperial Rome and one of his gimmicks is the existence of a fith court, the Marble Court, which acts as a sort of cultural counterpat to the seasonal courts. Its details, in short, are the following:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cinder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/play-by-post/1331503-ooc-dark-eras-changeling-vae-victis#post1331503\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The biggest difference when it comes to the Lost in Rome would be the existence of the Marble Court, an organization born during Republican times which quickly rose to prominence. The Marble Court embodies a simple truth: while changelings triumph and die, the Lost Courts of Rome endure eternal. The Marble Court is a reflection of the Roman ideal of conquest and stability. Its core belief is that the greatness of Rome is a blessing changeling should claim as their birthright, that by giving their trust to the traditions and structure which allowed Rome to prosper the Lost honor the incorruptible foundation that allows all of them to exist in safety. It is an ideal which found its natural evolution with the rise of imperial power and nowadays is pretty much the changeling\u2019s response to the cult of the empire. Seasons might change, but the marble under the Lost\u2019s feet stands untouched. Technically, the Marble Court has no right to rule, but the change of seasons cannot happen unless the Court formally allows it, with proper rituals and investitures. The Marble Court can keep the passage of crowns on hold and \u201cdutifully govern Rome\u2019s Lost until the situation returns to acceptable conditions\u201d. Its is a huge social and political circumstance which does not happen often and rarely lasts more than some weeks, but makes it so the Marble Court remains just as an important player in Roman Lost\u2019s politics as any other, perhaps even more so. The ancient oaths which empower the Marble Court seem to play an important part in Rome\u2019s protection from the Gentry, as shown by what happened when seasonal monarchs tried to govern without the Marble Court\u2019s blessing. The responsibility to decide whether or not to allow the passage of seasons lies within the hands of the most respected changelings of the Court, who behave as their position requires more often than not, but also means their criteria can be painfully mundane, ranging from old grudges to simple corruption. The Wyrd, as always, does not care for such banal, human details</div> </div> </div> </div>I originally planned to send it only to my players, though since I do think the results are decent enough to submit to criticism, I though that sharing its write-up here could not hurt. There's some stuff I'm not writing here <i>yet </i>because it counts as spoilers (not to mention I'll need to write a bunch of Contracts as well), but here's a start. Let me know what you think about it!<br /> <br /> <div align=\"center\"><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The Marble Court</b></span></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Amethyst Laurel, The Court of the Eagle and the Gladius</span></div> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>The hunter lowers his spear. As it touches the marble under the hunter\u2019s feet, the iron tip of his weapon makes a blunt sound which reverberates in the silence of the night. He looks at his surroundings. Behind him, a heavy door locked from the other side. Around him, people in full armor drawing closer, their weapons pointed at him. Either soldiers or mercenaries, working for someone with enough gold to pay for their time and experience. Above him, on the seating of the small arena he found himself trapped in, a dozen of changeling looking below. They stare in silence. Among all them, sitting in the prestige box, his quarry.<br /> He stands up. The light of the torches shines through his crystalline skin as he moves, but true light bathes the place only when he starts to talk. \u201cYou thought me to be an easy prey, didn\u2019t you, fiend? That I would flee as I did from the mines of your master? But here,\u201d he says while making a wide gesture to the whole audience, \u201chere you\u2019re in Rome. And I\u2019m Lucius Cornelius Scipio. My ancestors triumphed over Hannibal at Zama. I\u2019m not going anywhere.\u201d</i><br /> <br /> <b>STRENGTH THROUGH PRIDE</b><br /> <br /> True strength does not lie in the fickle, transient qualities of a single individual. It is what happens when such qualities are put into service of something greater, another stone in walls of a bastion which entire generations lived to preserve. Men and women die for these ideas, armies go to war and world changes, so it\u2019s hard to deny their power, and no idea proved to have more power than the glory that is Rome.<br /> <br /> Changelings are citizens of the empire just as any other person born on this side of the Hedge and, as such, they can draw from the strength of Rome should they prove up to the task. Such strength does not come easily: it\u2019s born out of honor, stoicism, and sacrifice. It\u2019s molded by traditions, structure, and abnegation. It comes with more duties than benefits, but blood and tears shed in its pursuit all contribute to the greater good. This is the eternal truth the Marble Court understands.<br /> <br /> Just as Rome endures thanks to its solid foundation, so do the Roman courts. The changelings of the seasonal courts might have a hard time admitting it, but without the stability offered by the Marble Court, there would be no Roman Lost. There would be no celebrations, no armies to move against the Gentry, no knowledge to preserve, and no safe havens to hide within. There would be changelings, perhaps, but they would be emotional, weak things, unworthy of being called Romans. It\u2019s the Marble Court that provides an unchanging center around which the turn of season can happen. With the Marble Court\u2019s changelings being the keepers of the traditions and qualities which allowed the Lost of Rome to prosper for this long, the very same beliefs which made Rome great in the first place, they exalt and encourage the other courts to be a better version of themselves.<br /> <br /> Being a member of the Marble Court grants comfort and a reason to be through the pride of being Roman. Its changelings see themselves of the inheritors of the armies who triumphed over Carthage, the birthplace of Caesar and Augustus, and the empire which unified so many different countries and people under a single banner. The Losts of the Marble Court are reassured that the traditions who helped to shape Rome will also protect and empower them, that the Roman way of life is the correct one.<br /> <br /> The Amethyst Laurel determines who is worthy of ruling over Rome, how to behave in time of crisis, and acts as impartial judge in matters of the utmost importance. The Marble Court might have no right to rule, but it\u2019s through its blessing others do and, should it need to take charge of Rome until other courts regain enough dignity, so be it. It is the Marble Court which reminds changelings of what is Rome and what is not, setting a boundary that even the Gentry must respect. When the Marble Court wields the duty of leadership, it imposes the ideals of Rome on the Gentry as well. The Fae, enemies at the gates who come from infinite kingdoms of thorns and madness, need to pay due respect to the empire if they wish to wage war to Rome when the Amethyst Laurel leads. The Others have to enact the proper rituals, make sacrifices, and even make tributes to changelings before the conflict can start for real. It\u2019s a declaration of intent accompanied by tangible efforts to ask for fate\u2019s approval for it. The mercurial nature of the Gentry is forced into a somewhat recognizable shape by centuries of Roman traditions, which often leads to jarring and bizarre results, but also forces the lords and ladies of Arcadia to ascribe to the changelings of Rome a degree of dignity and prideful importance because they are citizens of the Roman empire, a truth so strong even the True Fae have to respect it.<br /> <br /> Changelings of the Marble Court harvest the pride which gives them strength by putting such strength into practice. They remind others authority and command are a birthright of theirs. By doing that, they behave as the representatives of the empire they\u2019re expected to be, an attitude which shaped everything that came before them and everything that will come later. It might be a source of trouble, with so many ready to hold a grudge when you show them their place in the world, but it\u2019s either for their own good or they\u2019re foes who deserve to be vanquished.<br /> <br /> <b>FANATISM</b><br /> <br /> Pride blinds. The belief that your way of life, your ancestry, traditions, and nation are not only valuable but the <i>only </i>correct way to exist, it\u2019s easy to dismiss everything else as inferior. At best, it\u2019s worthless babble which wastes your time. At worst, it\u2019s something which menaces to poison everything you value from within.<br /> <br /> The Marble Court is the worst enemy of itself. It takes pride in its stability and firm strength, but such pride also leads towards self-destruction and stagnancy. After all, if the Roman way is the right one, then there\u2019s no reason to adapt to the passage of time or take some effort to reconsider how to behave. If it worked in the past, it\u2019s going to work forever. It\u2019s how we\u2019ll make it work now. This treacherous way of thinking lends itself both to corruption and mental closure, flaws which are going to eventually cause enough cracks in the foundation of the Marble Court for it to collapse on itself. Empty shows of force like games and parades matter little if everything is crumbling and those with the power to do something about it refuse to see it.<br /> In the meantime, this attitude also translates into not-so-veiled hostility towards all who dare to criticize the methods of the Amethyst Laurel or the actions of its members. To attack them means to attack Rome itself, regardless of the intentions of the opposite part, and to attack Rome marks someone as coward, pariah, traitor. Enemy of the empire.<br /> <br /> The Marble Court owes more than it is willing to admit to the Imperial cult and it\u2019s not always clear what it\u2019s true and what is propaganda. Courtiers themselves, even the most respected ones, often lack the necessary perspective to deal with challenges with the objectivity they require. They might allow other courts to make their voices heard but, when it comes critical decisions, it\u2019s their way or nothing.<br /> <br /> Another ugly byproduct of the Marble Court\u2019s dogma is how exclusively Roman it is. To say only Lost of pure Latin ancestry are allowed in the Amethyst Laurel would be a lie, but it\u2019s also true the Marble Court is the less cosmopolite court <i>by far</i> and that its members who come from noble Roman families are seen as first among equals, even inside a court which already considers itself as such. It should not be like this: every durance is different but all have the dubious virtue of making it possible for changelings to relate to each other from a shared suffering, to rebuild a new life from themselves regardless of who they were before the Thorns tore away their soul. But old habits die hard and there\u2019s still a distinction between who is Lost and who is instead a Roman Lost. It veers into true xenophobia at times, a fear which has consequences and whom enemies can use to deliver insidious but fatal wounds.<br /> <br /> The most subtle weakness of the Amethyst Laurel is another one, though. With its changeling being so enraptured by the noble tradition they\u2019re part of, their individuality often pays a price. If all that matters is Rome, a dream which will outlast them all, then what does a single person matter? Just as it happens for humans, the empire thrives thanks to sacrifices, control, and conquest. Rome does not care about the scars a changeling bears. It does not care about its dreams, its fears, nor about its enemies. If offers cold strength, sure, but sometimes those who are Lost need more a warm embrace and someone who does not consider their emotions as a weakness to be ashamed of.<br /> <br /> <b>Mantle: Marble</b><br /> <br /> <i>Mantle Effects: </i>The Marble Mantle is magnificent and imposing. It speaks of strength and honor, of glorious aggression and blood spilled by great man. Each step made while wearing it is made of solid ground with the uplifting certainty it will lead towards a great destiny. The sound of marching armies and triumphant music accompany it. The clothes it wraps around shine like gold. Wherever the courtier goes, he is never alone: he is of Rome and Rome walks with him.<br /> <br /> Recover a Glamour point whenever you establish dominance over people with different allegiances from yours.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Gain bonus dice equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots on mundane rolls to convince others you\u2019re the right person to take charge of the situation at hand.<br /> \u2022\u2022 Gain the benefits of the Gentrified Bearing Merit even if your character does not qualify.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter, you may spend a Willpower point to grant your character additional dots of Court Goodwill Merit equal to her Mantle dots, to a maximum of five per court, for the scene.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Your character can regain one Willpower point whenever he regains Glamour thanks to the Marble Mantle.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter, you may spend 1 Glamour and roll Mantle + Wyrd vs. Composure + Wyrd to inflict the Awestruck Condition on a target for the remainder of the scene.</span><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/815620-cinder-s-comprehensive-collection-of-creations\" target=\"_blank\">Cinder's Comprehensive Collection of Creations</a> - Homebrew Hub<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1172470-cinder-writes-about-beast-the-primordial\" target=\"_blank\">I write about Beast: The Primordial a lot</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","name":"Cinder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar38_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1408"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T14:43:14-07:00","id":1332735,"title":"","content":"P.S. Writing about an organization in love with the ideals of ancient Rome draws some unfortunate parallels here and there. Parallels I know far too well as an Italian dude with a tiny bit of historical self-awareness. In case it needs to be said, I don't endorse that crap. Whether its brand is distinctly Roman or any sort of &quot;The Empire We Used To Be&quot; people seem unable to let go.<br /> <br /> I'm fairly certain this was an unneeded addendum, but I'd rather be clear and direct when it comes to certain things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/815620-cinder-s-comprehensive-collection-of-creations\" target=\"_blank\">Cinder's Comprehensive Collection of Creations</a> - Homebrew Hub<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1172470-cinder-writes-about-beast-the-primordial\" target=\"_blank\">I write about Beast: The Primordial a lot</a>","edited":{"userid":38,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","user":"Cinder","date":". 08-26-2019, 05:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","name":"Cinder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar38_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1408"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}